    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V16: The split-ring DC motor. How does a split-ring motor keep turning? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Let's name the parts. The magnets provide the magnetic field. The coil, also called the armature, carries the current, and it's free to turn on an axle. At one end of the axle is the split-ring commutator. It's a ring cut into two halves, and each half is joined to one end of the coil, so the halves turn with the coil. Pressing on the ring are two brushes. They stay still, and they carry current from the supply into whichever half they're touching. Now look at the coil end on, along the axle. Its two long sides cross the magnetic field at right angles. Current comes out of the screen in one side, and goes into the screen in the other. As we've seen, each side feels a force at right angles to both the current and the field. And because the currents run opposite ways, the forces point opposite ways, one up, one down. Two equal, opposite forces, a distance apart. That pair turns the coil. The short end sides don't help, because their forces just push along the axle. How strong is that turning effect? You've met torque before: it's r F sine theta. Each long side is half the coil's width from the axle, and with n turns, its force is n I l B. Here's the neat part. The angle that matters turns out to be the angle between the field, and the coil's area normal, the arrow at right angles to its face, the same normal we used for flux. Add the two sides, and the width times the length is the area. Cos is close, so the turning part of each force, opposite theta, takes sine. So the torque is n I A B sine theta. That's the form we'll use, with theta measured from the normal.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Here's the simple motor from last time, running from the lab power pack. Its coil spins, round and round. Now let's change one thing. Take away the split ring, and connect the coil's two ends straight to the supply. Switch it on. Will it keep spinning, rock back and forth and stop, or not move at all? Make your prediction. It swings round, overshoots, swings back, and stops, with its face towards the magnets. Put the split ring back, and it spins again. So how does a split-ring motor keep turning? Let's name the parts. The magnets provide the magnetic field. The coil, also called the armature, carries the current, and it's free to turn on an axle. At one end of the axle is the split-ring commutator. It's a ring cut into two halves, and each half is joined to one end of the coil, so the halves turn with the coil. Pressing on the ring are two brushes. They stay still, and they carry current from the supply into whichever half they're touching. Now look at the coil end on, along the axle. Its two long sides cross the magnetic field at right angles. Current comes out of the screen in one side, and goes into the screen in the other. As we've seen, each side feels a force at right angles to both the current and the field. And because the currents run opposite ways, the forces point opposite ways, one up, one down. Two equal, opposite forces, a distance apart. That pair turns the coil. The short end sides don't help, because their forces just push along the axle.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v15's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"; the painted motor `y12p-dc-motor-model-v1.png` beside the lab power pack); the chip becomes the heading: \"How does a split-ring motor keep turning?\". On \"Its coil spins\" the amber rotation arrow circulates; amber hand tag \"spins\". On \"Take away the split ring\" the plate cross-fades to the coded three-quarter motor schematic at the same size (pole blocks \"N\" left, \"S\" right, slate field lines, rectangular coil on an axle, split ring and brushes at the front); the split ring and brushes fade out and two coded leads run straight to the coil's ends (red hand tag \"no split ring\"). On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside chips \"keeps spinning\", \"rocks and stops\", \"doesn't move\". On \"magnets\" the coil swings about 120°, overshoots, swings back with smaller swings and settles face on to the poles; the \"rocks and stops\" chip ticks and a red pen ring circles the settled coil. On \"again\" the split ring and brushes return, and the coil spins continuously with the amber rotation arrow. No hand appears; the supply switch on the pack changes state.",
    "understandingCheck": "Here's the simple motor from last time, running from the lab power pack. Its coil spins, round and round. Now let's change one thing. Take away the split ring, and connect the coil's two ends straight to the supply. Switch it on. Will it keep spinning, rock back and forth and stop, or not move at all? Make your prediction. It swings round, overshoots, swings back, and stops, with its face towards the magnets. Put the split ring back, and it spins again. So how does a split-ring motor keep turning? Your turn. A rectangular coil of one hundred and twenty turns measures four point zero centimetres by two point five centimetres. It carries one point five amps in a uniform field of zero point two five tesla. Calculate the torque when the plane of the coil is at sixty degrees to the field. Pause here to answer. Last one. Explain the function of the split-ring commutator in a simple DC motor. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V16: The split-ring DC motor. How does a split-ring motor keep turning? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 190 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,450 characters, 16 scenes, about 550 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v15's final frame.\n\nScope: sixteenth video of Year 12 Physics Module 6 (Electromagnetism, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"How has knowledge about the Motor Effect been applied to technological advances?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m6-v07.md` and `phys-y12-m6-v12.md`; `math-a12-d10.md` for the calculation layout. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lessons read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extracts `work/brief168/module6-lesson09.sa.txt` and `module6-lesson10.sa.txt`, hashes checked against the live files): lesson 09: orient and set convention (prediction, warm-up, intentions with τ = nBAI cos θ, vocabulary, misconceptions), derive and calculate torque (horseshoe-magnet loop, τ = nBAI cos θ, Figure 1, worked example 50 turns, MC), analyse the radial field (Figure 2, commutator, HSC tip, true/false), investigate and map angles (interactive, equivalent conventions τ = nBIA sin α), apply torque calculations (current and resistance condition, Activity 1), respond and consolidate (Activity 2, synthesis, copy into books, odd one out, \"Analyse 6 marks\", \"Account 5 marks\"), practice (Q1, Q2 and model answers), review; lesson 10: orient and compare (prediction, warm-up, intentions, vocabulary, misconceptions), DC structure and commutation (motor card, field and coil, contacts and switching, radial-field model, Stop and Check, MC), back emf overview, induction motor overview, compare and select, apply and consolidate, practice, review: `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module6/lesson09.sa.html`, SHA256 `511aaa66aed6d07ee4cb4a816dfa98a00e6972e247c67cdcb9d1971db16575ff`, and `.../lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `736384e8c6b6ea3e705767d723a0ee76e77ccceae7ab2d40f7f297065de8d36f` (both recomputed 6 Nov; match the route). Route entry: `phys-y12-m6-v16`, sections `derive-and-calculate-torque` (lesson 09) and `dc-structure-and-commutation` (lesson 10). Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Connect two wire forces to loop torque nIAB sin theta with the area-normal convention; explain commutator reversal and dead points. Stop before back emf.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m6-v17: Why does a spinning DC motor draw less current?\"\n\nOwner rules applied: as v07 and v12 (2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines: no hand rule is drawn, the force direction is given by v07's labelled coded axes and the coil is set turning by the current alone; the demonstration motor and the lab power pack are painted plates (one new, one reused), scaled uniformly, never stretched; the end-on coil, field, current symbols, force arrows, area normal, lever arms, torque graph, commutator close-up and calculation strips are coded, HSC style; B along the screen shown by slate lines, current out of the screen a dot and into the screen a cross; every number recalculated in Python; syllabus and data-sheet symbols τ = nIA⊥B = nIAB sin θ, F = lIB sin θ; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes). Recalled, not re-taught: v07 (the motor effect F = lIB sin θ, force at right angles to I and B, direction from the coded axes), v09 (the area normal and θ measured from it; \"Face on, full flux. Edge on, no flux.\"), Module 5 v09 (torque τ = rF sin θ, \"Push square, push far.\"), Year 11 (rotational inertia in words: a turning object keeps turning).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 6; ledger id `P6-IQ4-01`), quoted from the route JSON `syllabusActions`:\n\n- `P6-IQ4-01`: \"investigate the operation of a simple DC motor to analyse:\" \"the functions of its components\", \"production of a torque τ=nIA_(⊥)B=nIABsinθ\", \"effects of back emf (ACSPH108)\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 57)\n\nThis video covers the first two examples; \"effects of back emf\" is v17's (route split). The ledger marks `P6-IQ4-01` as a practical (\"investigate\"); the route lists no practical for v16, so the investigation is left to the practical plan (open decision 3).\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v15's final frame (power point, lab power pack, leads to a simple DC motor whose coil spins, \"?\" over the split ring and brushes). Teaches: the observation (a coil connected straight to the supply rocks and stops face on to the poles; with the split ring it keeps turning); the parts (field magnets, coil, axle, split-ring commutator, brushes) and their functions; the two long sides carry opposite currents, so they feel opposite forces (v07), a pair that turns the coil; the end sides give no turning effect; the torque of the pair from τ = rF sin θ: τ = nIAB sin θ, θ between the area normal and B, A⊥ = A sin θ; maximum edge on, zero face on (the dead point), the contrast with flux; the site worked example (50 turns, 4.0 cm × 3.0 cm, 3.0 A, 0.20 T: 3.6 × 10⁻² N m; plane at 30° to B, so θ = 60°: 3.1 × 10⁻² N m); the plane-angle trap; without a commutator the torque reverses past the dead point, so the coil rocks and stops; the split ring reverses the current every half turn, so the torque keeps the same sense; inertia carries the coil through the dead point; several coils and a radial field smooth the torque (named). Stops before back emf, the current drawn and stall current (v17), and AC motors and generators (v18 onwards).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How does a split-ring motor keep turning?\" is causal. The answer, built in order: two opposite forces on the coil's sides make a torque (3, 4); that torque falls to zero face on and would reverse after it (5, 7); the split ring swaps the coil's connections at that moment, reversing the current, so the forces swap and the torque keeps turning the same way (8); the coil's inertia carries it through the zero (8).\n- **Hook (site lesson 09 Analyse step 5 and lesson 10 \"hidden problem\"; original staging).** v15's motor, then the same coil wired straight to the supply (no split ring; coded leads straight to the coil's ends, which can only swing so far). 3 s prediction: \"keeps spinning\", \"rocks then stops\", \"doesn't move\". The coil swings, overshoots, rocks back and settles face on to the poles. Back on the split ring, it spins. This makes the split ring's job the question.\n- **Parts and functions (syllabus \"the functions of its components\"; site lesson 10 \"Field and coil\", \"Contacts and switching\").** Named on the new painted demonstration motor plate with pen arrows: field magnets (provide B), coil or armature (carries the current that feels the forces), axle (lets it turn), split-ring commutator (two half rings that turn with the coil), brushes (fixed sliding contacts that carry current to the turning ring). \"Armature\" given as the other name once.\n- **Forces on the sides (v07 recalled).** End-on coded view: the two long sides cross the field at right angles; their currents run opposite ways (one out of the screen, one in), so their forces are opposite (amber, up and down). The short end sides: their forces act along the axle and give no turning effect (site Analyse step 2). Direction from v07's coded axes, \"thumb I, fingers B, palm F\" named in words only (root decision 3).\n- **Torque derivation (route \"nIAB sin theta with the area-normal convention\"; Module 5 v09 and v09 recalled).** Each long side is r = w/2 from the axle and feels F = nIlB (n turns, v07's F = lIB with n wires). The angle between r and F equals θ, the angle between the area normal and B (shown by rotating both arrows together; author note a). τ = 2 × (w/2) × nIlB × sin θ = nI(lw)B sin θ = nIAB sin θ. On screen also \"= nIA⊥B, A⊥ = A sin θ\" so the data-sheet form is recognised (author note b).\n- **Edge on and face on (new contrast with v09's aid).** θ = 90° (normal across the field, the coil's plane along the field, \"edge on\"): torque maximum, nIAB. θ = 0° (normal along the field, \"face on\"): the forces pull along the coil's plane through the axle, torque zero: the dead point. Spoken contrast: in video nine, face on gave full flux; here face on gives no torque. Site misconception: \"Maximum flux corresponds to zero torque\".\n- **Angle convention (site lesson 09 uses the plane angle and cos; open decision 1).** The video uses the syllabus and data-sheet form, θ from the area normal. One sentence in scene 6 warns that some books measure the angle from the coil's plane and use cos, so \"always check which angle\". The site's worked example (θ = 30° between the plane and B) is presented as \"the plane is at thirty degrees to the field, so the normal is at sixty\", giving the site's 3.1 × 10⁻² N m.\n- **Worked example (site lesson 09, unchanged values).** 50 turns, 4.0 cm × 3.0 cm, 3.0 A, 0.20 T: A = 1.2 × 10⁻³ m², τ_max = 3.6 × 10⁻² N m; plane at 30° to B: θ = 60°, τ = 3.6 × 10⁻² × sin 60° = 3.1 × 10⁻² N m. Two stages, area conversion in the givens.\n- **The problem and the fix (route \"explain commutator reversal and dead points\"; site lesson 10 DC structure; lesson 09 radial field card).** Without a commutator, past the dead point the same current gives the same force directions, but the sides have swapped over, so the torque turns the coil back: it rocks and settles (author note c, friction). With the split ring, each half ring is joined to one end of the coil and turns with it; the brushes stay put; at the dead point the brushes cross the gaps and swap halves, so the current in the coil reverses, the forces reverse, and the torque keeps its sense. A τ against angle graph changes from a sine wave (rock) to a train of positive humps (spin). The coil's inertia carries it across the dead point, while the brushes briefly touch the gaps (no current) (author note d).\n- **Smoothing (site HSC tip: radial field and commutator; lesson 10 \"multiple coils\").** One scene: a coil stopped exactly at the dead point will not start by itself, and the torque pulses; real motors use several coils at different angles (with more commutator segments), and curved pole pieces with an iron core make a radial field that keeps the sides edge on to the field, so the torque stays near maximum. Named and shown, not derived (site lesson 09 `analyse-radial-field` is outside the route sections; open decision 2).\n- **Memory aid (scene 10): \"Every half turn, flip the current.\"** See \"Memory aid check\". The edge on and face on contrast is the scene 5 tag, \"Face on, no torque. Edge on, full torque.\", and also appears in the key notes as a second line, not as a second aid.\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): 120 turns, 4.0 cm × 2.5 cm, 1.5 A, 0.25 T, plane at 60° to the field: θ = 30°, τ = 0.045 × 0.50 = 2.3 × 10⁻² N m (2.25 × 10⁻²). Crossed: using sin 60° (3.9 × 10⁻² N m). A2 (3 marks): explain the function of the split-ring commutator. Crossed: \"it changes AC into DC\" (the site's own MC distractor \"Convert AC to DC\").\n- **Significant figures.** Site values as given (3.6 × 10⁻², 3.1 × 10⁻² N m); A1 to two significant figures (2.3 × 10⁻² N m; 2.25 × 10⁻² shown as the unrounded value).\n- **Handoff (route next).** Scene 15 returns to the lab power pack and the spinning motor with a coded ammeter in the supply lead: at switch-on the needle jumps high, and as the coil speeds up the needle settles lower; \"?\" between. \"Why does a spinning DC motor draw less current?\". No values, no back emf named. See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key (root decision 1):** magnetic field slate lines; current orange (dots and crosses in the end-on view orange); force amber; area normal navy; lever arm r ink dashed; torque graph amber; rotation arrows amber outline; brushes grey; split ring copper.\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above; θ arcs and n, A givens navy; τ values and the torque readout amber; framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current working line at least 44 px (scene 4's derivation, scene 6 and the feedback rows 52 px; framed \"τ = nIAB sin θ\" 60 px); live readouts (scene 5 θ, sin θ and τ/τ_max; scene 8 current direction) about 52 px; part labels on the plate 34 px; supporting lines, graph axis labels, givens strips and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** The delivered painted motor `y12p-dc-motor-model-v1.png` (brief 190, inspected: base board, red and blue magnets, copper coil on an axle, two copper half rings with gaps, two brushes) is on screen from the first frame (v15's final frame) and fills scene 2 for the parts; the lab power pack carries the handoff.\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is scene 5's angle drag on the end-on coil: θ sweeps 90°, 60°, 30°, 0° while the lever arms shrink, a point rides the τ against θ graph and live readouts update (θ; sin θ 1.00, 0.87, 0.50, 0; τ/τ_max the same). Scenes 1, 7 and 8 keep the rotation model with inertia (rocking without the split ring, spinning with it) and scene 8's linked commutator close-up.\n- **Stage changes (Revision 2).** Revision 1 kept the end-on coil for scenes 3 to 5 (about 114 s). Now scene 3 is the end-on coil with forces; scene 4 a clear change to the one-side derivation stage (one side enlarged with r, F and both arcs, algebra at the right); scene 5 the end-on coil with the live graph. Other stages: plate (2), working layout (6), end-on rocking (7), split view (8), three-coil and radial views (9). No set-up runs past about 60 s.\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a small diagram in one move. On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data, the diagram shows the answer, results are framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 09: horseshoe-magnet loop and \"couple\" (3); vocabulary \"Torque\", \"Number of turns\", \"Split-ring commutator\", \"Radial magnetic field\" (2, 4, 8, 9); misconceptions 1 and 2 (5, 6); worked example (6); Figure 1's end-on view and force pair at three angles (3, 5); \"Equivalent angle conventions\" (6); \"Analyse 6 marks\" steps 2 to 5 (which sides act, the couple, inertia, commutator) (3, 7, 8); \"Account 5 marks\" (radial field) (9); MC 60° (A1 style). Lesson 10: DC motor card (\"hidden problem\", half rings, brushes) (1, 2, 8); \"Field and coil\", \"Contacts and switching\" (2); MC \"purpose of the split-ring commutator\" (A2 crossed). Not used: back emf, stall current, induction motors (v17 onwards); the resistance claim in Q2 (v17's territory).\n- **Site notes (quoted from site lessons 09 and 10, read 6 Nov; for root to log in `../site-corrections-phys-y12.md` if accepted):**\n  1. Error (lesson 09, \"Analyse 6 marks\" steps 3 and 6): \"the torque is $\\tau = nIAB\\sin\\theta$ in the form used here\", \"with $\\theta$ the angle between the plane of the coil and the field\". With θ measured from the plane, the torque is nIAB cos θ (the page's own convention); sin θ needs θ from the area normal. The answer then says the torque is maximum \"when the plane of the coil lies along the field\", where its own formula gives zero. Correction: \"τ = nIAB sin θ, with θ the angle between the area normal and the field\" (syllabus form), or keep cos θ with the plane angle throughout.\n  2. Error (lesson 09, \"Account 5 marks\" steps 2, 4 and 5): \"$\\tau = nIAB\\sin\\theta$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between the plane of the coil and the field direction\" and \"The field is then always in the plane of the coil, so $\\theta$ stays at $90^\\circ$\". If the field lies in the plane, the plane angle is 0°, not 90°. Correction: define θ as the angle between the area normal and the field; then a radial field keeps θ at 90° and sin θ at 1.\n  3. Inconsistency (lesson 09, throughout): the page teaches τ = nBAI cos θ with the plane angle, while the syllabus and data sheet give τ = nIA⊥B = nIAB sin θ. The page's \"Equivalent angle conventions\" note is correct; suggest leading with the syllabus form (θ from the area normal) and giving the cos form as the alternative, so students meet the data-sheet form first.\n  4. Mismatch (lesson 09, review): \"At the start you were asked about a 50-turn coil of area 2.0 cm² carrying 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ A in a 0.15 T field\". The opening prediction asks a different, qualitative question, and 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ A is implausible for a coil (probably a typing error). The arithmetic for the stated values is correct (1.5 × 10⁻¹³ N m). Correction: align the review with the opening prediction, and use a realistic current (for example 1.0 A gives 1.5 × 10⁻³ N m).\n  5. Precision note (lesson 10, `dc-structure-and-commutation`): \"the brushes switch from one half-ring to the other ... every half-turn\" is right; the card's \"as the coil turns through 180°, the forces on its sides would naturally reverse\" is loose: without the commutator the forces keep their directions, and the torque reverses because the sides have changed places. Suggest \"as the coil turns past the face-on position, the torque would reverse and turn it back\".\n  6. Checked and correct: worked example (1.2 × 10⁻³ m², 3.6 × 10⁻² N m, 3.1 × 10⁻² N m); MC (0.50 τ_max at a 60° plane angle); Activity 1 (2.4 × 10⁻² N m; 1.7 × 10⁻² N m at 45°; fill-the-gap 10.0 mN m); Q1 (0.12 N m); lesson 10 worked example and practice (1.0 A, 6.0 A; 1.0 A; 5.0 A, 12 V; 9 V and 6 V).\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) without a split ring the coil rocks and stops; with it, it spins (1); (2) the long sides carry opposite currents across the field, so they feel opposite forces (3); (3) the pair of forces, a distance apart, makes a torque, τ = nIAB sin θ (4); (4) the torque is largest edge on and zero face on (5); (5) past face on, the same current would turn the coil back (7); (6) the split ring reverses the current at face on, so the torque keeps its sense, and inertia carries the coil through (8); (7) more coils and a radial field keep the torque large and steady (9).\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v07 (motor effect), v09 (area normal), Module 5 v09 (torque); Year 11: current, circuits, a turning object keeps turning unless something stops it.\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** DC motor (a device that uses the motor effect to turn electrical energy into rotation, 1); coil or armature (the loop of wire that turns, 2); split-ring commutator (a ring cut into two halves joined to the coil's ends, 2); brushes (fixed contacts that press on the turning ring, 2); torque (the turning effect of a force, recalled, 4); area normal (an arrow at right angles to the coil's face, recalled, 4); dead point (the face-on position where the torque is zero, 5); radial field (a field that points along the coil's plane wherever it turns, 9).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. The coded family `m6-coil-torque` (\"Force pair turns a coil\"; limits: \"End-on section; angle measured from coil plane; commutator omitted\") supplies the end-on section and force pair; its angle readout must be changed to the area-normal convention, and the commutator is added (kit gap 1).\n- **New explainer motion:** **end-on coil** (a coded end-on section: the axle as a small circle, the coil as a line through it with a dot side and a cross side, slate field lines left to right, amber force arrows on each side, the navy area normal, ink dashed lever arms, an angle arc for θ between the normal and B, a turning angle driven by a simple rotation model with inertia and a little friction); **commutator close-up** (front view of the split ring: two copper half rings with gaps, two grey brushes, orange current arrows through brush, half ring and coil, the current reversing as the gaps pass the brushes, linked to the end-on coil's angle); **torque graph** (`DataGraph` τ against rotation angle, drawn live with a cursor: a sine wave without the commutator, positive humps with it, a flatter band for several coils); **parts plate with pen arrows**; `AlignedAlgebra`; `NumberTicker`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 11 and 13); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the end-on coil left of centre; the commutator close-up or graph to the right; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) End-on geometry: axle at the origin, coil plane along unit vector u, area normal n ⟂ u, B along x. The sides sit at ±(w/2)u; the forces are along ±y (⟂ B and ⟂ the current along the axle). The angle between u and y equals the angle between n and x, so each side's torque is (w/2)F sin θ with θ between the normal and B. Two sides: τ = wF sin θ = nIlwB sin θ = nIAB sin θ.\n  (b) A⊥ in the data-sheet form τ = nIA⊥B: read as A sin θ, the component of the area vector (A along the area normal) at right angles to B. The narration does not interpret A⊥; the screen shows \"A⊥ = A sin θ\" only.\n  (