motor card, field and coil, contacts and switching, radial-field model, Stop and Check, MC), back emf overview, induction motor overview, compare and select, apply and consolidate, practice, review: `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module6/lesson09.sa.html`, SHA256 `511aaa66aed6d07ee4cb4a816dfa98a00e6972e247c67cdcb9d1971db16575ff`, and `.../lesson10.sa.html`, SHA256 `736384e8c6b6ea3e705767d723a0ee76e77ccceae7ab2d40f7f297065de8d36f` (both recomputed 6 Nov; match the route). Route entry: `phys-y12-m6-v16`, sections `derive-and-calculate-torque` (lesson 09) and `dc-structure-and-commutation` (lesson 10). Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Connect two wire forces to loop torque nIAB sin theta with the area-normal convention; explain commutator reversal and dead points. Stop before back emf.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m6-v17: Why does a spinning DC motor draw less current?\"\n\nOwner rules applied: as v07 and v12 (2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines: no hand rule is drawn, the force direction is given by v07's labelled coded axes and the coil is set turning by the current alone; the demonstration motor and the lab power pack are painted plates (one new, one reused), scaled uniformly, never stretched; the end-on coil, field, current symbols, force arrows, area normal, lever arms, torque graph, commutator close-up and calculation strips are coded, HSC style; B along the screen shown by slate lines, current out of the screen a dot and into the screen a cross; every number recalculated in Python; syllabus and data-sheet symbols τ = nIA⊥B = nIAB sin θ, F = lIB sin θ; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes). Recalled, not re-taught: v07 (the motor effect F = lIB sin θ, force at right angles to I and B, direction fro