ertia carries the coil through the dead point; several coils and a radial field smooth the torque (named). Stops before back emf, the current drawn and stall current (v17), and AC motors and generators (v18 onwards).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How does a split-ring motor keep turning?\" is causal. The answer, built in order: two opposite forces on the coil's sides make a torque (3, 4); that torque falls to zero face on and would reverse after it (5, 7); the split ring swaps the coil's connections at that moment, reversing the current, so the forces swap and the torque keeps turning the same way (8); the coil's inertia carries it through the zero (8).\n- **Hook (site lesson 09 Analyse step 5 and lesson 10 \"hidden problem\"; original staging).** v15's motor, then the same coil wired straight to the supply (no split ring; coded leads straight to the coil's ends, which can only swing so far). 3 s prediction: \"keeps spinning\", \"rocks then stops\", \"doesn't move\". The coil swings, overshoots, rocks back and settles face on to the poles. Back on the split ring, it spins. This makes the split ring's job the question.\n- **Parts and functions (syllabus \"the functions of its components\"; site lesson 10 \"Field and coil\", \"Contacts and switching\").** Named on the new painted demonstration motor plate with pen arrows: field magnets (provide B), coil or armature (carries the current that feels the forces), axle (lets it turn), split-ring commutator (two half rings that turn with the coil), brushes (fixed sliding contacts that carry current to the turning ring). \"Armature\" given as the other name once.\n- **Forces on the sides (v07 recalled).** End-on coded view: the two long sides cross the field at right angles; their currents run opposite ways (one out of the screen, one in), so their forces are opposite (amber, up and down). The short end sides: their forces act along the axle and give no turning effect (site Analyse step 2)