e same force directions, but the sides have swapped over, so the torque turns the coil back: it rocks and settles (author note c, friction). With the split ring, each half ring is joined to one end of the coil and turns with it; the brushes stay put; at the dead point the brushes cross the gaps and swap halves, so the current in the coil reverses, the forces reverse, and the torque keeps its sense. A τ against angle graph changes from a sine wave (rock) to a train of positive humps (spin). The coil's inertia carries it across the dead point, while the brushes briefly touch the gaps (no current) (author note d).\n- **Smoothing (site HSC tip: radial field and commutator; lesson 10 \"multiple coils\").** One scene: a coil stopped exactly at the dead point will not start by itself, and the torque pulses; real motors use several coils at different angles (with more commutator segments), and curved pole pieces with an iron core make a radial field that keeps the sides edge on to the field, so the torque stays near maximum. Named and shown, not derived (site lesson 09 `analyse-radial-field` is outside the route sections; open decision 2).\n- **Memory aid (scene 10): \"Every half turn, flip the current.\"** See \"Memory aid check\". The edge on and face on contrast is the scene 5 tag, \"Face on, no torque. Edge on, full torque.\", and also appears in the key notes as a second line, not as a second aid.\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): 120 turns, 4.0 cm × 2.5 cm, 1.5 A, 0.25 T, plane at 60° to the field: θ = 30°, τ = 0.045 × 0.50 = 2.3 × 10⁻² N m (2.25 × 10⁻²). Crossed: using sin 60° (3.9 × 10⁻² N m). A2 (3 marks): explain the function of the split-ring commutator. Crossed: \"it changes AC into DC\" (the site's own MC distractor \"Convert AC to DC\").\n- **Significant figures.** Site values as given (3.6 × 10⁻², 3.1 × 10⁻² N m); A1 to two significant figures (2.3 × 10⁻² N m; 2.25 × 10⁻² shown as the unrounded value).\n- **Handoff (route next).** Scene 15 