ut the split ring, spinning with it) and scene 8's linked commutator close-up.\n- **Stage changes (Revision 2).** Revision 1 kept the end-on coil for scenes 3 to 5 (about 114 s). Now scene 3 is the end-on coil with forces; scene 4 a clear change to the one-side derivation stage (one side enlarged with r, F and both arcs, algebra at the right); scene 5 the end-on coil with the live graph. Other stages: plate (2), working layout (6), end-on rocking (7), split view (8), three-coil and radial views (9). No set-up runs past about 60 s.\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a small diagram in one move. On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data, the diagram shows the answer, results are framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 09: horseshoe-magnet loop and \"couple\" (3); vocabulary \"Torque\", \"Number of turns\", \"Split-ring commutator\", \"Radial magnetic field\" (2, 4, 8, 9); misconceptions 1 and 2 (5, 6); worked example (6); Figure 1's end-on view and force pair at three angles (3, 5); \"Equivalent angle conventions\" (6); \"Analyse 6 marks\" steps 2 to 5 (which sides act, the couple, inertia, commutator) (3, 7, 8); \"Account 5 marks\" (radial field) (9); MC 60° (A1 style). Lesson 10: DC motor card (\"hidden problem\", half rings, brushes) (1, 2, 8); \"Field and coil\", \"Contacts and switching\" (2); MC \"purpose of the split-ring commutator\" (A2 crossed). Not used: back emf, stall current, induction motors (v17 onwards); the resistance claim in Q2 (v17's territory).\n- **Site notes (quoted from site lessons 09 and 10, read 6 Nov; for root to log in `../site-corrections-phys-y12.md` if accepted):**\n  1. Error (lesson 09, \"Analyse 6 marks\" steps 3 and 6): \"the torque is $\\tau = nIAB\\sin\\theta$ in the form used here\", \"with $\\theta$ the angle between the plane of the coil and the field\". With θ measured from the plane, the torque is nIAB cos θ (the page's own convention); sin θ needs θ from the area normal. The answer then says the torque is maximum \"when the plane of the coil lies along the field\", where its own formula gives zero. Correction: \"τ = nIAB sin θ, with θ the angle between the area normal and the field\" (syllabus form), or keep cos θ with the plane angle throughout.\n  2. Error (lesson 09, \"Account 5 marks\" steps 2, 4 and 5): \"$\\tau = nIAB\\sin\\theta$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between the plane of the coil and the field direction\" and \"The field is then always in the plane of the coil, so $\\theta$ stays at $90^\\circ$\". If the field lies in the plane, the plane angle is 0°, not 90°. Correction: define θ as the angle between the area normal and the field; then a radial field keeps θ at 90° and sin θ at 1.\n  3. Inconsistency (lesson 09, throughout): the page teaches τ = nBAI cos θ with the plane angle, while the syllabus and data sheet give τ = nIA⊥B = nIAB sin θ. The page's \"Equivalent angle conventions\" note is correct; suggest leading with the syllabus form (θ from the area normal) and giving the cos form as the alternative, so students meet the data-sheet form first.\n  4. Mismatch (lesson 09, review): \"At the start you were asked about a 50-turn coil of area 2.0 cm² carrying 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ A in a 0.15 T field\". The opening prediction asks a different, qualitative question, and 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ A is implausible for a coil (probably a typing error). The arithmetic for the stated values is correct (1.5 × 10⁻¹³ N m). Correction: align the review with the opening prediction, and use a realistic current (for example 1.0 A gives 1.5 × 10⁻³ N m).\n  5. Precision note (lesson 10, `dc-structure-and-commutation`): \"the brushes switch from one half-ring to the other ... every half-turn\" is right; the card's \"as the coil turns through 180°, the forces on its sides would naturally reverse\" is loose: without the commutator the forces keep their directions, and the torque reverses because the sides have changed places. Suggest \"as the coil turns past the face-on position, the torque would reverse and turn it back\".\n  6. Checked and correct: worked example (1.2 × 10⁻³ m², 3.6 × 10⁻² N m, 3.1 × 10⁻² N m); MC (0.50 τ_max at a 60° plane angle); Activity 1 (2.4 × 10⁻² N m; 1.7 × 10⁻² N m at 45°; fill-the-gap 10.0 mN m); Q1 (0.12 N m); lesson 10 worked example and practice (1.0 A, 6.0 A; 1.0 A; 5.0 A, 12 V; 9 V and 6 V).\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) without a split ring the coil rocks and stops; with it, it spins (1); (2) the long sides carry opposite currents across the field, so they feel opposite forces (3); (3) the pair of forces, a distance apart, makes a torque, τ = nIAB sin θ (4); (4) the torque is largest edge on and zero face on (5); (5) past face on, the same current would turn the coil back (7); (6) the split ring reverses the current at face on, so the torque keeps its sense, and inertia carries the coil through (8); (7) more coils and a radial field keep the torque large and steady (9).\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v07 (motor effect), v09 (area normal), Module 5 v09 (torque); Year 11: current, circuits, a turning object keeps turning unless something stops it.\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** DC motor (a device that uses the motor effect to turn electrical energy into rotation, 1); coil or armature (the loop of wire that turns, 2); split-ring commutator (a ring cut into two halves joined to the coil's ends, 2); brushes (fixed contacts that press on the turning ring, 2); torque (the turning effect of a force, recalled, 4); area normal (an arrow at right angles to the coil's face, recalled, 4); dead point (the face-on position where the torque is zero, 5); radial field (a field that points along the coil's plane wherever it turns, 9).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. The coded family `m6-coil-torque` (\"Force pair turns a coil\"; limits: \"End-on section; angle measured from coil plane; commutator omitted\") supplies the end-on section and force pair; its angle readout must be changed to the area-normal convention, and the commutator is added (kit gap 1).\n- **New explainer motion:** **end-on coil** (a coded end-on section: the axle as a small circle, the coil as a line through it with a dot side and a cross side, slate field lines left to right, amber force arrows on each side, the navy area normal, ink dashed lever arms, an angle arc for θ between the normal and B, a turning angle driven by a simple rotation model with inertia and a little friction); **commutator close-up** (front view of the split ring: two copper half rings with gaps, two grey brushes, orange current arrows through brush, half ring and coil, the current reversing as the gaps pass the brushes, linked to the end-on coil's angle); **torque graph** (`DataGraph` τ against rotation angle, drawn live with a cursor: a sine wave without the commutator, positive humps with it, a flatter band for several coils); **parts plate with pen arrows**; `AlignedAlgebra`; `NumberTicker`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 11 and 13); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the end-on coil left of centre; the commutator close-up or graph to the right; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) End-on geometry: axle at the origin, coil plane along unit vector u, area normal n ⟂ u, B along x. The sides sit at ±(w/2)u; the forces are along ±y (⟂ B and ⟂ the current along the axle). The angle between u and y equals the angle between n and x, so each side's torque is (w/2)F sin θ with θ between the normal and B. Two sides: τ = wF sin θ = nIlwB sin θ = nIAB sin θ.\n  (b) A⊥ in the data-sheet form τ = nIA⊥B: read as A sin θ, the component of the area vector (A along the area normal) at right angles to B. The narration does not interpret A⊥; the screen shows \"A⊥ = A sin θ\" only.\n  (c) Without a commutator (coil leads free to twist a little, or slip rings on DC), the coil oscillates about the face-on position (θ = 0°, stable) and friction brings it to rest there. θ = 180° is also a zero-torque position but unstable.\n  (d) At the dead point the brushes bridge or touch the gaps; with a two-segment commutator the current is briefly zero or the coil briefly short-circuited, depending on brush width. Not taught; the narration says \"for a moment no current flows\".\n  (e) Rotational inertia is not in the Year 12 syllabus as a formula; the narration says \"the coil is already turning, so it keeps going through that point\".\n  (f) Pronunciations: \"commutator\" (KOM-yoo-tay-tuh); \"armature\"; \"theta\"; \"N I A B sine theta\".\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. Angle convention (root, science check).** This video uses only the syllabus form (θ from the area normal, sin θ), with one warning sentence about the plane angle and cos. The site uses the plane angle and cos throughout lesson 09 (site note 3). Keep (recommended: matches the data sheet and v09), or present both forms equally.\n  - **2. Smoothing scene (9) (learning review).** Several coils and the radial field go slightly beyond the route sections but are the site's HSC tip. Keep as one short scene, or cut to a key note if the voiced take runs long.\n  - **3. The \"investigate\" practical (root, coverage check).** `P6-IQ4-01` is flagged as a practical; the route assigns none to v16 or v17. Decide whether a short practical video (building a simple motor, varying current and field) is needed for coverage.\n  - **4. New plate `y12p-dc-motor-model-v1` (Codex).** Closed in Revision 2: delivered in brief 190 and inspected.\nMain question: *Wire a coil straight to a battery between two magnets, and it rocks and stops. Add a split ring, and it spins. What does the split ring do?*\nEntry knowledge: motor effect (v07); area normal (v09); torque (Module 5 v09).\nStops after: parts and functions, th